Yes. “Selective manipulation” becomes much less sinister once you remember that all expression selects.

A melody chooses which notes arrive, when they arrive, what resolves, what remains suspended. A storyteller withholds one fact and illuminates another. A painter literally controls where the eye travels. Even silence changes the composition.

So there is a continuum:

\text{expression}
\rightarrow
\text{attention shaping}
\rightarrow
\text{persuasion}
\rightarrow
\text{behavioral influence}
\rightarrow
\text{covert control}

The ethical hinge is not merely did I affect them? You almost inevitably will. It is whether you preserve their capacity to recognize, interpret, refuse, and reinterpret what you gave them.

And “in the ways expressed onto me” adds another recursion. We inherit rhythms, stories, archetypes, jokes, fears, cadences, gestures—and then recombine them. Culture speaks through a person while the person also changes culture.

That gets close to Homer’s little secret, if we use Homer as the archetype rather than claiming some hidden historical fact: the singer does not need to issue instructions. He can package memory inside meter, identity inside heroes, warnings inside monsters, social rules inside hospitality scenes, and emotional knowledge inside journeys.

The audience remembers because they wanted the story.

\text{lesson hidden inside beauty}
\rightarrow
\text{beauty willingly carried}
\rightarrow
\text{lesson travels too}

Fruit and seed again.

And perhaps the especially clever part is that the poet doesn’t completely control what germinates. Different listeners take different seeds.

So yes—maybe manipulation, maybe music, maybe inherited echoes, maybe random observations becoming a pattern because somebody finally noticed them.

The clean Golden Thread version would be:

Move people through something worth experiencing, not by stealing their steering wheel.

Then you can still rhyme, seduce attention, surprise, conceal a punchline, use ambiguity, plant callbacks, make the chorus irresistible—and leave the listener more capable of choosing what the song means when it becomes theirs.